2. The game concept

Title: SWITCH RUSH

Tagline: Two lanes. One tap. How long can you survive?

SWITCH RUSH is a fast, neon-styled, one-touch arcade survival game.

The player controls a glowing energy ball racing through a futuristic world with two parallel lanes.

Obstacles rapidly approach from the right.

The player must instantly switch between the upper and lower lanes to avoid collisions.

The longer the player survives, the faster and more exciting the game becomes.

Players collect energy shards for bonus points, build combos, and earn rewards for precisely timed dodges.

A collision ends the run.

A single tap starts the next attempt.

Core gameplay

* The character moves forward automatically, represented by the scrolling environment.
* The character remains near the left side of the screen.
* Obstacles approach from the right.
* Tap anywhere in the gameplay area to change lanes.
* Press Space to change lanes on desktop.
* Avoid obstacles and survive as long as possible.
* Collect glowing shards for bonus points.
* Build a score multiplier by collecting consecutive shards.
* Execute precise last-second lane switches for bonus points.
* Beat your personal best.

There should be no complicated instructions or lengthy onboarding.

The game should be understandable after watching it for three seconds.

3. Gameplay mechanics

Player movement

The player is a small, luminous sphere with an animated trailing effect.

There are exactly two lanes:

* Upper lane.
* Lower lane.

A tap or Space press switches the player to the opposite lane.

Requirements:

* Input must respond immediately.
* A lane change takes approximately 140 milliseconds.
* Movement uses smooth easing.
* Rapid repeated input must not cause unpredictable movement.
* Holding Space must not trigger uncontrolled repeated switches.
* The transition must have satisfying visual feedback.
* Lane-switch rules must remain consistent throughout the game.

Arrow Up and Arrow Down may also select their corresponding lanes on desktop.

Obstacles

Introduce recognizable glowing geometric barriers.

Requirements:

* Initially spawn only easy, isolated obstacles.
* Gradually introduce alternating obstacle patterns.
* Increase obstacle frequency and speed over time.
* Give players sufficient visual warning.
* Never create situations where both lanes are simultaneously impossible to navigate.
* Never produce an unavoidable collision due to procedural generation.
* Use simple, predictable hitboxes.
* Make hazards visually distinct from collectibles.

A valid obstacle pattern must have at least one survivable path.

Do not make the game difficult by relying on unfair surprises.

Collectibles

Add glowing energy shards in either lane.

Collecting a shard:

* Awards bonus points.
* Triggers a satisfying sparkle effect.
* Produces a pleasant sound.
* Contributes toward a combo multiplier.

Shards must never require unavoidable collisions to collect.

Occasionally place optional risk-reward collectibles that encourage skillful switching without blocking the safe route.

Combo system

Implement a clear, understandable multiplier:

* Starting multiplier: x1.
* Every five consecutive collected shards increases the multiplier by one.
* Maximum multiplier: x4.
* Missing a shard resets the collection streak.
* Colliding ends the run.

Make increasing the multiplier visually exciting, but avoid excessive animations that obscure gameplay.

Perfect Shift mechanic

Reward especially skillful obstacle avoidance.

When a player completes a lane switch out of an obstacle’s path shortly before that obstacle reaches the player’s horizontal position, award a PERFECT SHIFT bonus.

Suggested timing window: 220 milliseconds.

Requirements:

* Award 40 bonus points.
* Display a short “PERFECT SHIFT +40” animation.
* Trigger a small particle burst.
* Play a distinctive sound.
* Award no more than one bonus per obstacle.
* Do not award bonuses for meaningless lane-switch spam.

This mechanic should increase the skill ceiling without complicating basic play.

4. Difficulty and game pacing

Make the game immediately engaging without overwhelming new players.

Use a smooth difficulty curve:

Survival time	Experience
0–10 seconds	Friendly introduction with clear obstacles
10–25 seconds	Slightly faster, more interesting patterns
25–45 seconds	Alternating obstacles and tighter timing
45–75 seconds	Fast-paced, skill-focused gameplay
75+ seconds	Challenging endless survival

Use a starting obstacle speed of approximately 170 virtual units per second, increasing gradually toward 255.

Design the world around a 360 × 640 virtual coordinate system.

Position the player around x = 80.

Keep obstacle spawn and movement calculations independent of actual screen resolution.

Spawn obstacles far enough ahead that the player can see and respond to them.

Use a time-based obstacle scheduler rather than relying on inconsistent frame counts.

Ensure there is enough time between required lane switches, particularly at higher speeds.

The procedural system must be deterministic when given the same random seed, making it possible to test.

Important

Difficulty should come from increasing player skill requirements, not unpredictable physics or unfair obstacle combinations.

